The Bearing column needs a whole-circle bearing from the Easting difference (D7) and the Northing difference (E7).

`ATAN2(E7,D7)` measures the angle from north towards east, so it returns the bearing directly. `MOD(...,360)` turns negative results into the 0–360° range. This gives the same result as the quadrant rules in your Key table. It also avoids a divide-by-zero error when the Northing difference is 0.

{"formula": "=MOD(DEGREES(ATAN2(E7,D7)),360)"}

Headwind is the wind speed times the cosine of the angle between the runway heading and the wind direction. I copied the angle expression from the crosswind formula in X10 so the two cells stay consistent. I left out ABS so a negative result shows a tailwind.

One thing to check: X10 wraps the angle in DEGREES(), but Excel's SIN and COS expect radians. The correct conversion is RADIANS(U7-X7), so both X9 and X10 may need fixing.

{"formula": "=X8*COS(DEGREES(U7-X7))"}

The Time Delta column (M) most likely mirrors the Cost Delta formula already in N2, `=ABS(E2-K2)`. Applying the same pattern to the time columns gives the absolute difference between Time A (D2) and Time B (J2).

One caveat: the times are shown as text (e.g. "Jan 5 2012 12:15:32.12"). If they are stored as text rather than real date-time values, the subtraction will return `#VALUE!`. In that case the values need converting to real date-times first.

{"formula": "=ABS(D2-J2)"}

G2 sits where the year 2019 (G1) meets quarter 1 (F2). Column C already holds a maximum price for each quarter, so G2 most likely wants the highest price in Q1 2019.

The formula uses `MAXIFS` to keep only sale dates from the first day of the quarter up to, but not including, the first day of the next quarter. The mixed references (`G$1` and `$F2`) let it be filled across to 2020 and down through quarters 2–4.

{"formula": "=MAXIFS($B:$B,$A:$A,\">=\"&DATE(G$1,($F2-1)*3+1,1),$A:$A,\"<\"&DATE(G$1,$F2*3+1,1))"}

The "no of occurences" column (E) should count how many rows in column B match each period in column D. Column B returns 5, 6, 7 or 0 for each date, and D9 holds "5 days ", so E9 should count the 5s.

`LEFT(D9,1)` pulls the digit from the label ("5"). `COUNTIF` treats that text criterion as the number 5. This means the formula can be filled down to E10 and E11 to count the 6s and 7s without any edits. I used the whole column `$B:$B` because the date list probably continues past row 22. B1 is blank, so it doesn't affect the count.

{"formula": "=COUNTIF($B:$B,LEFT(D9,1))"}

Row 24 lists shift start times, and A25 holds employee "A". Employee "A" is most likely the schedule row just above row 5, which would be row 4 (it isn't shown in the context). So the formula counts how many of A's shifts across columns B–L start at the time in B24.

The `"*"` wildcard matches values like "06:00-14:00". In K24, "Off" with the wildcard also catches OFF, OFF/VAC and OFF/STAT, because COUNTIF ignores case. The mixed references let the formula copy across time columns and down employee rows.

{"formula": "=COUNTIF($B4:$L4,TEXT(B$24,\"hh:mm\")&\"*\")"}

The Movement Date in A2 is text such as "Mon, 27 Feb 23 18:26:52 GMT", so the Date column most likely needs that text turned into a real date. `MID(A2,6,9)` takes the "27 Feb 23" part, which starts after "Mon, ", and `DATEVALUE` converts it to an Excel date.

This assumes the day is always two digits. A single-digit day like "3 Mar 23" would need a length-flexible version.

{"formula": "=DATEVALUE(MID(A2,6,9))"}

Column J is labelled "month payed", so J3 most likely finds the pay month for the completed date in I3. The In (from) dates in B3:B23 are in ascending order, so `LOOKUP` can find the latest pay-period start that is on or before I3 and return the matching month from column A. For example, I3 (2011-11-11) falls after B3 (2011-10-23), so it returns A3 (2011-11-01).

If I3 is blank or earlier than the first start date, this returns `#N/A`.

{"formula": "=LOOKUP(I3,$B$3:$B$23,$A$3:$A$23)"}

The "Date Value" column most likely pulls just the date out of the "First Join" timestamp in C2. These timestamps look like text from a meeting attendance export (e.g., "11/20/23, 2:02:13 PM"). The formula takes everything before the comma and converts it to a date with DATEVALUE.

If C2 actually holds a real date/time value rather than text, `=INT(C2)` would give the same result.

{"formula": "=DATEVALUE(LEFT(C2,FIND(\",\",C2)-1))"}

The Date column is text like "Fri 14 Mar 7:50pm", so D2 most likely needs to pull out the "14 Mar" part and turn it into a real date. Column E will probably hold the time separately. B2 and C2 already read D2 for the day and month names.

The formula starts at character 5, just after "Fri ". It stops at the space after the month, which it finds from the third space in the text. That way it also works for single-digit days such as "1 Mar". DATEVALUE then converts the result to a date. Because the text has no year, Excel will assume the current year.

{"formula": "=DATEVALUE(MID(A2,5,FIND(\" \",A2,FIND(\" \",A2,5)+1)-5))"}

Column P is labelled DELTA, and the sheet already computes the Black-Scholes d1 in N2 and gamma in Q2 with a dividend-yield discount. So P2 most likely holds the call delta: e^(−q·t)·N(d1). Here q is O2, t is M2, and N(d1) is the standard normal cumulative distribution of N2.

I used `NORMSDIST`, the older form, to match the sheet's older function style. `NORM.S.DIST(N2,TRUE)` gives the same result in newer Excel.

{"formula": "=EXP(-1*M2*O2)*NORMSDIST(N2)"}

The label "interpolation" in I7 suggests this cell should linearly interpolate the climb table. My best guess is that it interpolates **time** at the field elevation in H2 (1500). This is an assumption, since the sheet doesn't say whether time, fuel or distance is wanted.

The formula finds the table row at or just below H2. It then uses FORECAST on that row and the next one (1000 and 2000, giving 2 for time). For fuel or distance instead, replace `C3` with `D3` or `E3`.

{"formula": "=FORECAST(H2,OFFSET(C3,MATCH(H2,B3:B15,1)-1,0,2),OFFSET(B3,MATCH(H2,B3:B15,1)-1,0,2))"}

Column L is headed "Punnam", one of the forum contributors' formulas being compared, and that formula isn't shown anywhere in the sheet. This completion is my best guess at it, not his exact formula.

It works out the hours between Start (E8) and End (F8) and handles shifts that cross midnight. If the end time is earlier than or equal to the start time, it adds one day (`F8<=E8` counts as 1). That means a 12:00 AM to 12:00 AM shift counts as 24 hours. The result is multiplied by 24 to give hours, like the other columns.

{"formula": "=(F8-E8+(F8<=E8))*24"}

The Hours column should be End minus Start, converted to hours. Several shifts end at 12:00 AM, which means they cross midnight. A few rows show 12:00 AM to 12:00 AM, which I read as full 24-hour shifts. So when End is at or before Start, the formula adds one day before multiplying by 24.

If 12:00 AM to 12:00 AM is meant to be 0 hours rather than 24, use `=MOD(F8-E8,1)*24` instead.

{"formula": "=(F8-E8+(F8<=E8))*24"}

The label in I25 reads "CURRENT HCI =", so J25 most likely calculates a golf handicap index. Under the World Handicap System, that is the average of the best (lowest) 8 score differentials from the last 20 rounds. Column G holds those differentials, and the last 20 visible rounds are rows 13–32.

If the data continues below row 32, the range would need to shift down to cover the latest 20 rounds.

{"formula": "=AVERAGE(SMALL(G13:G32,{1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8}))"}

The "Previous Years of Experience" values in column B are text (for example "14 years 9 months "), so this formula pulls the years and months out of that text. It converts them to total months and adds the months of service from the hire date up to the benchmark date in D1. The result is shown in the same "X years, Y months" style as column D.

Note: `LET` needs Excel 365 or Excel 2021 or later.

{"formula": "=LET(m,LEFT(B4,FIND(\" \",B4)-1)*12+MID(B4,FIND(\"years\",B4)+6,FIND(\" months\",B4)-FIND(\"years\",B4)-6)+DATEDIF(C4,$D$1,\"m\"),INT(m/12)&\" years, \"&MOD(m,12)&\" months\")"}

B17 should hold the whole-year part of the average account age, to pair with C17.

C17 uses `MOD(...,12)` to get the leftover months. The same expression divided by 12 and wrapped in `INT` gives the full years. That expression is the total age in months (years × 12 + months) divided by the number of accounts.

{"formula": "=INT(((SUM(B2:B7)*12+SUM(C2:C7))/ROWS(A2:A7))/12)"}

Column G appears to mirror column O on the right-hand side, so G5 likely takes the LARGE version of the F5 formula using the same A4:D4 range and C5 comparison. This is a best guess from the layout.

I left out the IFERROR wrapper your note asks for. If you want 0 instead of an error, use `=IFERROR(LARGE(A4:D4,COUNTIF(A4:D4,">"&C5)+1),0)`.

{"formula": "=LARGE(A4:D4,COUNTIF(A4:D4,\">\"&C5)+1)"}

Column B repeats some KP values, so one KP can match several speed readings. Column D lists each KP once at 1m steps. The likely intent is to average the speeds in column A for each KP in D, which is what AVERAGEIF does. I used whole-column references because the data length isn't known.

Note that some D values carry floating-point noise (e.g. D15 is 70.5170000000001). An exact match could miss those, so wrapping D3 in ROUND(D3,3) may be safer.

{"formula": "=AVERAGEIF($B:$B,D3,$A:$A)"}

The "Match" header in I9 suggests that I10 should find where the Start year and calendar week (G10 and H10) appear together in the data. This formula returns the position of the first row where Year equals 2020 and CalendarWeek equals 5. You can then use that position with INDEX to pull the value.

I assumed the data runs no further than row 1000. In older Excel versions you may need to enter it as an array formula with Ctrl+Shift+Enter.

{"formula": "=MATCH(1,($B$3:$B$1000=G10)*($A$3:$A$1000=H10),0)"}

The header for L reads "BOTH / 2ND SCORE", which most likely means the best second-place score from either series. This is an interpretation, so check it against how the sheet is meant to score. Following the pattern already used in J and K, the formula:

- takes the 2nd-largest WAGC score and the 2nd-largest CMT score for the row;
- returns whichever is higher;
- uses `IFERROR` to return 0 when a series doesn't have two scores.

{"formula": "=MAX(IFERROR(LARGE(IF($B$2:$I$2=J$2,$B4:$I4),2),0),IFERROR(LARGE(IF($B$2:$I$2=K$2,$B4:$I4),2),0))"}

For C21, this takes 25% of the lower of cost and deemed cost. When deemed cost is 0, it uses cost instead. The 25% rate comes from B25, locked as `$B$25` so the formula can be copied across to D21:F21.

{"formula": "=IF(C20=0,C19,MIN(C19:C20))*$B$25"}

The Income Tax Value formula in C22:F22 (`=MIN(C19:C20)-C21`) doesn't handle the zero case. In F22 it would take MIN(1300000, 0) = 0 and give a negative result. Changing it to `=IF(C20=0,C19,MIN(C19:C20))-C21` would fix that.

This formula counts the working days where the row's start–end period (A24:B24) overlaps the month in column D (D20:D21). It then multiplies that count by the utilization in C24. The outer MAX(0,…) returns 0 when the period doesn't overlap the month at all.

The mixed references ($A24, D$20) mean it can be filled across and down. This is a guess at your intent: if you only want the month's working days times utilization, the simpler `=D$22*$C24` matches your earlier rows.

{"formula": "=MAX(0,NETWORKDAYS(MAX($A24,D$20),MIN($B24,D$21)))*$C24"}

The layout sets up a percentile calculation. J1 holds the year and J2 the month. Column E keeps a day's value only when it matches that year and month and is at least 1; otherwise it returns "". J3 holds the percentile (95), so J4 most likely calculates that percentile of the included values.

The formula divides J3 by 100 because `PERCENTILE` expects a fraction. It uses the whole column E:E because the data's last row isn't visible (1980–2009 daily data would run to about row 10,959). The "Include" header and the "" cells are text, which `PERCENTILE` ignores in a range. Since I4 has no label, this is an inference rather than a certainty.

{"formula": "=PERCENTILE(E:E,J3/100)"}

The labels in A1:A7 follow the argument order of Excel's `PRICE` function exactly (settlement, maturity, rate, yield, redemption, frequency, basis), so B9 most likely calls `PRICE` on those cells.

`PRICE` assumes the redemption value is quoted per $100 of face value. B5 is 1000, so the result may not be the dollar price you expect. If you want the price of a $1,000 bond, set redemption to 100 and multiply the result by 10.

{"formula": "=PRICE(B1,B2,B3,B4,B5,B6,B7)"}

M22 is the Accumulated Depreciation for an asset using the straight-line (SLN) method. This matches the formulas in M19 and M21, so it follows the same pattern.

One caveat: this asset was bought in 2009 with a 10-year life, so it is already fully depreciated. This formula will report more depreciation than cost minus salvage. To cap it, you could use `=MIN(SLN(E22,F22,G22)*I22,E22-F22)` instead.

{"formula": "=SLN(E22,F22,G22)*I22"}

I3 is "Total Work Hours". The neighbouring cells follow a pattern: each summary column pulls from the 'WR Data' column one letter to its right (J→K, K→L, L→M, and so on). Following that pattern, I3 would sum 'WR Data' column J. The same closed-date (column G) month criteria as the other SUMIFS cells apply.

This is an inferred guess: the context doesn't show which 'WR Data' column actually holds work hours, so check that column J is correct.

{"formula": "=SUMIFS('WR Data'!$J$3:$J$3996, 'WR Data'!$G$3:$G$3996, \">=\"&DATE($B$2, A3, 1), 'WR Data'!$G$3:$G$3996, \"<=\"&EOMONTH(DATE($B$2, A3, 1), 0))"}

The Provider 1 formula in D2 shifts down 11 rows per provider (`INT(ROW()-2)/2*11` gives 11 at row 4). So I've assumed Provider 2's tracker block starts at row 21 on 'PTO Tracker 2025', with its leave codes in C21:C23. If the blocks are spaced differently, the row numbers will need adjusting.

The formula follows the same pattern as D2: whole days ×8, ×8 and half days ×4, counted over the month's 6×7 calendar grid.

{"formula": "=COUNTIF(OFFSET('PTO Tracker 2025'!$F$21,0,(COLUMN(A$1)-1)*8,6,7),'PTO Tracker 2025'!$C21)*8+COUNTIF(OFFSET('PTO Tracker 2025'!$F$21,0,(COLUMN(A$1)-1)*8,6,7),'PTO Tracker 2025'!$C22)*8+COUNTIF(OFFSET('PTO Tracker 2025'!$F$21,0,(COLUMN(A$1)-1)*8,6,7),'PTO Tracker 2025'!$C23)*4"}

This assumes H should rebuild the "Desired %" ramp in column L, so the O-column checks (`=H2=L2`) return TRUE. Each month steps evenly from the previous phase's % (0 for Phase 1) up to its own phase's % in `$B$8:$B$11`. It reaches the full value in the phase's last month. For example, Phase 1 gives 0.05 then 0.1, and Phase 2 gives about 0.15, 0.2, 0.25.

The step uses the month's position within its phase divided by the number of months in that phase. It needs `LET`, so it won't work in older Excel versions. Floating-point results may not match column L exactly, and your note in Q7 about rounding may apply.

{"formula": "=LET(p,MATCH(G2,$A$8:$A$11,0),cur,INDEX($B$8:$B$11,p),prev,IF(p=1,0,INDEX($B$8:$B$11,p-1)),prev+(cur-prev)*COUNTIF($G$2:G2,G2)/COUNTIF($G$2:$G$17,G2))"}

H9 sits under the "n" header, so it most likely counts how many values in column B fall between "from" (F9) and "to" (G9). This counts values greater than or equal to the start and below the end, so neighbouring ranges don't count the same value twice.

One catch: the last range ends at `MAX(B:B)` (G11), so that row would need `"<="&G11` instead, or the maximum value won't be counted.

{"formula": "=COUNTIFS(B:B,\">=\"&F9,B:B,\"<\"&G9)"}

This formula assumes you want the first-year budget prorated by the months remaining after the start date. That's a guess based on the full dates in C2 and C3, which fall mid-year.

The formula looks up the start year (from C2) in the Year/Budget table (G2:H26). It then multiplies that budget by the share of the year from the start month onward: 6/12 for a July start, giving 50 for 2015. If you only want the full budget for the start year, drop the `*(12-MONTH($C$2)+1)/12` part.

{"formula": "=VLOOKUP(YEAR($C$2),$G$2:$H$26,2,FALSE)*(12-MONTH($C$2)+1)/12"}

Cell D31 sits under the "Total Bonus" header, so this formula sums the bonus amounts for each closing that falls in the row's month and the year in A29. It mirrors the closings count in C31.

I'm assuming the bonus amounts are in columns F, K, P and U. Each of these sits just before its closing-date column (G, L, Q, V). The data repeats in 5-column blocks, and F and K hold numbers like 111 and 298. P and U aren't visible in the sheet extract, so check that they hold the bonus amounts.

{"formula": "=SUMPRODUCT(--($G$2:$G$27<>\"\"),--(MONTH($G$2:$G$27)=(ROW()-ROW($A$30))),--(YEAR($G$2:$G$27)=A$29),$F$2:$F$27)+SUMPRODUCT(--($L$2:$L$27<>\"\"),--(MONTH($L$2:$L$27)=(ROW()-ROW($A$30))),--(YEAR($L$2:$L$27)=A$29),$K$2:$K$27)+SUMPRODUCT(--($Q$2:$Q$27<>\"\"),--(MONTH($Q$2:$Q$27)=(ROW()-ROW($A$30))),--(YEAR($Q$2:$Q$27)=A$29),$P$2:$P$27)+SUMPRODUCT(--($V$2:$V$27<>\"\"),--(MONTH($V$2:$V$27)=(ROW()-ROW($A$30))),--(YEAR($V$2:$V$27)=A$29),$U$2:$U$27)"}

D2 is read by `INDIRECT($D$2)` in B2 and C2, so it most likely needs to return a cell address as text. B2 and C2 then shift that address one and two columns right (into L and M). So D2 should give the address in column K of the row matching A2.

One caveat: A2 currently holds "A", while K2:K10 holds the numbers 1–9. As written, the MATCH would return #N/A until A2 contains a value from column K.

{"formula": "=CELL(\"address\",INDEX($K$2:$K$10,MATCH($A$2,$K$2:$K$10,0)))"}

The cell Q3 has no label, so I'm guessing what you intend. The sheet has three blocks, each a label followed by a formula cell: G3/H3, J3/K3 and M3/N3. Q3 sits right after the last one (M3/N3), whose formula is still "?". That block asks for a distinct count of column B values where column C matches C3, column D is "Medium" and column E is "Within".

I adapted the working formula from H3 by adding the "Medium" and "Within" conditions. The two words are typed directly into the formula because the sheet doesn't show cells holding them.

{"formula": "=SUM(IF(FREQUENCY(IF(SUBTOTAL(3,OFFSET(B6,ROW(B6:B18)-ROW(B6),,1)),IF((C6:C18=C3)*(D6:D18=\"Medium\")*(E6:E18=\"Within\"),MATCH(B6:B18,B6:B18,0))),ROW(B6:B18)-ROW(B6)+1),1))"}

The header row C1:M1 holds the numbers 1 to 11, and column B holds monthly dates. That layout most likely means each cell should show the date that many months after the row's date. This is a guess based on the layout alone, since nothing else on the sheet confirms it.

`$B2` keeps the column fixed on the date, and `C$1` keeps the row fixed on the month offset. That way the formula can be filled across and down the grid. The result will show as a serial number until the cell is formatted as a date.

{"formula": "=EDATE($B2,C$1)"}

The sheet lists stock tickers down column A and months 1–11 across row 1. That suggests each cell should hold that ticker's monthly closing price. This formula uses STOCKHISTORY with a monthly interval (`2`) and no headers (`0`), so it returns only the closing value.

The year 2024 is an assumption because the sheet doesn't show one; change it if you need a different year. `$A3` stays on the ticker column and `B$1` stays on the month row, so the formula can be filled across and down.

{"formula": "=STOCKHISTORY($A3,DATE(2024,B$1,1),DATE(2024,B$1,1),2,0)"}

B20 sits in the "Average" row, so it most likely averages the monthly values Jan–Dec in B6:B17.

H1 and H2 hold row numbers 6 and 17, so you might instead want a dynamic version: `=AVERAGE(INDIRECT("B"&$H$1&":B"&$H$2))`. I've gone with the simpler direct range.

{"formula": "=AVERAGE(B6:B17)"}

The row label in F5 is "Events Open", so G5 most likely counts events raised in G3's month that have status "Open". This follows the pattern already used in G7, which filters by the same date window and status (G7 also filters by classification).

I used whole-column references to match G7. G4 uses the fixed range `$A$5:$A$11` instead, which would miss rows 12–13.

{"formula": "=COUNTIFS($A:$A,\">=\"&G$3,$A:$A,\"<=\"&EOMONTH(G$3,0),$B:$B,\"Open\")"}

The block starting at row 15 mirrors the one starting at row 6. In the first block, AD7 counts stage-2 marks with `"st2"`. In the second block, AC16 counts stage 1 with the number `1` instead of `"st1"`. So AD16 most likely counts stage 2 using the Stage 2 date in Z16 and the number `2`.

One uncertainty: rows 17 and 19 show `1` even where the first block had `st2`. The second block may therefore use `1` for every stage. If so, the last argument should be `1` rather than `2`.

{"formula": "=COUNTIFS($C$15:$W$15,\"<=\"&Z16,$C16:$W16,2)"}

C430 is the first empty cell in column C, so it should follow the pattern from the rows above, with row references updated to 430.

{"formula": "=IFERROR(TIMEVALUE(RIGHT(A430,LEN(A430)-FIND(\"4 \",A430)-1)),\"\")"}

The search string is "4 " because it matches the end of the year "2024" in A430 ("Sent: 02 January 2024 13:18"). That string won't match rows from 2023, such as A431, so those rows would return blank. I've kept it as is to match your existing formulas.

F3 should return the easting from the old data (column L) of the old point closest to the new point in row 3. E3 already uses `MATCH($F3,$L:$L,0)`, so F3 must hold an exact value from column L.

The formula finds the smallest squared distance, (L−B3)²+(M−C3)², and returns the matching L value. It needs dynamic arrays (Excel 365 or 2021); in older Excel, enter it with Ctrl+Shift+Enter.

{"formula": "=INDEX($L$3:$L$21,MATCH(MIN(($L$3:$L$21-$B3)^2+($M$3:$M$21-$C3)^2),($L$3:$L$21-$B3)^2+($M$3:$M$21-$C3)^2,0))"}

Cell H3 sits under the "Out" header, paired with the "In" column G for the date in G1. The likely intent is to mirror G3's formula, using MAX instead of MIN and the Out times in column C instead of B. This returns the latest Out time for that person on that date.

`G$1` stays relative in the column, so copying H3 to J3 would point it at I$1, the next date.

{"formula": "=MAX(IF($A$2:$A$16=$F3, IF(ISNUMBER(SEARCH(G$1,$D$2:$D$16)),$C$2:$C$16)))"}

Row 37 is labelled "XIRR" in A37, and row 36 holds the current values dated with `=TODAY()`, so B37 most likely calculates the XIRR for column B. The cash flows are in column B and the dates are in column A.

Only rows 17–37 are visible, so I've assumed the data starts at row 2 under a header in row 1. If it starts elsewhere, adjust the range. The date range is absolute (`$A$2:$A$36`) so the formula can be copied across columns C to L.

{"formula": "=XIRR(B2:B36,$A$2:$A$36)"}

For "Length of Project (in months)", `DATEDIF` with the `"m"` unit counts the complete months between the start date in E4 and the end date in F4.

For 1 Dec 2023 to 31 May 2024, this returns 5. If you want partial months to count (giving 6), add `+1` to the end.

{"formula": "=DATEDIF(E4,F4,\"m\")"}

The value in I3 (86.668) equals the average of row A's three figures: (118.847 + 66.657 + 74.5) / 3 = 260.004 / 3 = 86.668. H1 asks for the output without referencing the spill (D3#). A plain range reference to the same cells, D3:F3, satisfies that.

I couldn't work out what J3 (64.4945) represents, so this formula only reproduces the I3 result.

{"formula": "=AVERAGE(D3:F3)"}
